 "note": "The rebuild's rig sockets (slice 4), in the game's sockets file shape: Unreal frame (X forward, Y starboard, Z up), centimetres, in the boat's frame. Not the game's file: slice 9 writes that. BandBoom and BandTop are the mast's black bands' inner edges (P), for c22_check.py. As in today's file, Chainplate{Upper,Lower}_* are the wires' feet (the turnbuckles' tops), ForestayTack and BackstayPlate the deck pins, LowerTang on the mast's centreline and MastBase the vang's foot on the after face. New: ChainplateLowerFwd_* (the game draws one lower a side, the aft one), ChainplatePin*_* (the chainplates' pins), CapTang_*, LowerTangFwd_*/LowerTangAft_* (where the model's wires end at the mast: the game draws the uppers from SpreaderTip to Hounds), MastHeel, and ForestayFoot/BackstayFoot (where the stays leave their turnbuckles: a live wire drawn to the deck pin would run through one).",
 "hardware_note": "Slice 6's hardware sockets (docs/c22-7), Unreal frame, centimetres: where the game's live ropes end. JibWinch_* is where the headsails' sheets come onto the drum (its starboard side: the winches turn clockwise), JibTailer_* where the tail leaves its top turn for the trimmer's hand and JibTailRest_* where it lies out of a hand (the cleat's hitch), JibLead_* the genoa's car's sheave (today's name), WorkingJibLead_*, JibFairlead_* and JibTurn_* the working jib's lead over the cabin top, Traveler_C the car at the centre and Traveler_S/P its ends' stops, LazySheetVia where the lazy sheet crosses forward of the mast, the *Cleat* sockets each cleat's hitch."
}